            "text": "At the edge of the city, where the last apartment blocks gave way to allotments and railway weeds, there was a bench facing west. It was not a remarkable bench. Its paint had peeled into pale curls, and one slat bowed slightly in the middle. Yet every evening, people arrived there with the careful punctuality of birds returning to a familiar wire.\n\nA nurse came first, usually still wearing blue scrubs beneath a coat. She ate an orange and placed the peel in a paper bag. An old man followed, carrying a radio that only received one station clearly. Then came two schoolchildren, who argued about planets, football, and whether clouds could remember shapes. They did not know one another well. They rarely exchanged more than greetings. But they shared the sunset as if it were a public utility.\n\nSome evenings the sky offered nothing dramatic: a gray wash, a few lights coming on in distant windows. On those days, the bench seemed to teach its quiet lesson most clearly. Attention did not need a reward. The nurse watched the rails shine after rain. The old man turned down his radio. The children fell silent when a fox crossed the grass.\n\nYears later, construction crews replaced the bench with a polished metal seat and a sign describing a new walking route. The people complained, briefly, then adapted. Still, the old man said the sunsets had changed. Nobody agreed, but nobody dismissed him either. They understood: places keep traces of us."
